<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-arclength" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Arclength</title>

  <p>Suppose we are given a curve <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> on the time interval 
  <m>a \leq t \leq b</m>.  How can we figure out how long this curve is?
  </p>

  <activity>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the line <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle 2,3\rangle+t\langle 4,-3\rangle</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
        How long is the line segment between <m>\vec{r}(0)</m> and <m>\vec{r}(1)</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
        How long is the line segment between <m>\vec{r}(3)</m> and <m>\vec{r}(5)</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
        Can you express this length in terms of <m>\vec{r}(3)</m> and <m>\vec{r}(5)</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <activity>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Now consider the curve <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle t^3-2t,2t^2\rangle</m>.
      </p>
      <image width="50%">
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-arclength-estimate">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/arclength-estimate.xml"/>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Estimate the length of the curve between <m>\vec{r}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)</m> and <m>\vec{r}(1)</m>.  
        </p> 
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          How might we make this estimate more accurate?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <p>
    Now, lets consider a curve <m>\vec{r}(t)</m>, and add up approximate arclengths
  </p>
  <image>
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-arclength">
      <xi:include href="prefigure/arclength.xml"/>
    </prefigure>
  </image>
  <p>So our arclength is approximated by
    <md>\left|\vec{r}(t_1)-\vec{r}(t_0)\right|+\cdots+\left|\vec{r}(t_8)-\vec{r}(t_7)\right|</md>, 
    which we might write more generally as
    <md>\sum _{i=0} ^n |\vec{r}(t_{i+1})-\vec{r}(t_i)|</md>.
  </p>
  <p>Observing that this is almost a difference quotient, we may rewrite and then
  apply the mean value theorem:
  <md>
    <mrow>\sum _{i=0} ^n |\vec{r}(t_{i+1})-\vec{r}(t_i)| \amp = \sum _{i=0} ^n \left| \frac{\vec{r}(t_{i+1})-\vec{r}(t_i)}{\Delta t}\right| \Delta t </mrow>
    <mrow> \amp = \sum _{i=0} ^n |\vec{r}^\prime(t_i^*)| \Delta t</mrow>
  </md>.
  Then, taking a limit as the size of the time intervals goes to zero, we have
  <md> \lim _{\Delta t \rightarrow 0} \sum _{i=0} ^n |\vec{r}^\prime(t_i^*)| \Delta t = \int _{a} ^b |\vec{r}^\prime(t)|\ dt</md>.
</p>

</section>